WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.440
Every protein in your body needs nitrogen, and so does every strand of DNA.

2
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.400
The air around you is mostly nitrogen gas.

3
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.440
So why can't a soybean, a deer, or you use a single breath of it?

4
00:00:18.383 --> 00:00:21.483
Nitrogen gas is locked for plants and animals.

5
00:00:22.743 --> 00:00:24.543
They cannot build with it in that form.

6
00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:28.443
Two other stores are locked for many living things.

7
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:31.383
One is a dead leaf on the ground.

8
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.723
The other is fiber, which your own body cannot digest.

9
00:00:36.883 --> 00:00:39.303
So who could possibly use nitrogen gas?

10
00:00:40.583 --> 00:00:43.163
Make your guess before we look inside a soybean root.

11
00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.070
Some bacteria can.

12
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.890
They live inside small bumps on soybean roots, called nodules.

13
00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.330
There they turn nitrogen gas into ammonium, a form the plant can use.

14
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.190
This is called nitrogen fixation.

15
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:06.950
The plant pays them back.

16
00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.610
Sugar made in the leaves by photosynthesis runs down the stem to the nodules.

17
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.570
Each partner gets something it needs.

18
00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:20.630
A partnership in which both species benefit is called mutualism.

19
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:24.860
Now the soybean dies, and its stem lies on the soil.

20
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.100
Bacteria and fungi grow on it.

21
00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:33.260
They release enzymes that break its large molecules into smaller ones.

22
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.100
Nutrients return to the soil.

23
00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.140
These microbes are decomposers.

24
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.360
The third store is inside you.

25
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.660
Bacteria in your large intestine break fiber down by fermentation, without oxygen.

26
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.080
They make small acids your body can absorb for energy.

27
00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:57.560
Three locked stores, three microbes that unlock them.

28
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:03.280
Each time, the microbe takes matter apart, and some of it is handed on to other

29
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.120
living things. That is why life depends on microbes.

30
00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.690
Now look at a grapevine.

31
00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:15.650
A bacterium called Xylella grows in its water-carrying tubes and blocks them.

32
00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:19.750
The arrow runs one way, from vine to bacterium.

33
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.370
A microbe that causes disease in its host is a pathogen.

34
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:29.013
The host is the living thing a pathogen lives in or on.

35
00:02:30.173 --> 00:02:33.553
Here, the pathogen grows inside its host and harms it.

36
00:02:34.673 --> 00:02:37.233
The vine gives, and gets nothing useful back.

37
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:40.853
So is a kind of microbe a helper or a harm?

38
00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:46.793
Take E. coli. Most strains live quietly in your large intestine.

39
00:02:48.173 --> 00:02:51.393
Other strains carry extra genes that make them harmful.

40
00:02:52.753 --> 00:02:56.173
People who swallow enough of them in food or water can get sick.

41
00:02:57.217 --> 00:02:58.497
Here is the pattern.

42
00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:04.197
Some microbes unlock matter that others cannot use alone, and hand it on.

43
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:06.657
Life depends on them.

44
00:03:07.977 --> 00:03:09.817
Pathogens cause disease in their hosts.

45
00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:15.777
Whether a microbe helps, harms or does neither can depend on its strain and where it

46
00:03:15.777 --> 00:03:16.037
lives.
